          style="font-weight:600; font-size:38px; line-height:1.1; letter-spacing:-.02em; margin:0 0 14px; max-width:780px; color:#F2FBFC;"
        >
          From design to running code in three steps.
        </h2>
        <p style="font-size:17px; line-height:1.6; color:#90A2AA; max-width:680px; margin:0 0 40px;">
          Moment is the first of three tools that take a domain from a design to verified code. Each
          one answers a single question, and each hands the next something it can check.
        </p>
        <ThreeTools />
        <div style="display:grid; grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr; gap:16px; margin-top:22px;">
          <div
            style="background:#0A0F14; border:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius:12px; padding:22px 24px;"
          >
            <div style="font-weight:600; font-size:15.5px; margin-bottom:7px; color:#E6F0F2;">
              Feature holds the code to the design
            </div>
            <p style="margin:0; font-size:13.5px; line-height:1.55; color:#90A2AA;">
              A <span style="font-family:'JetBrains Mono',monospace;font-size:12.5px;color:#C9E8ED;"
                >.feat</span
              > file is an execution specification for one command, query, policy or projection. It
              tells the builder, a person or an AI agent, what to build and what to refuse. It
              predicts every observable effect: the response, the events, the records written. Then
              it compiles into a complete test suite. An effect the spec did not predict is a
              failure.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div
            style="background:#0A0F14; border:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius:12px; padding:22px 24px;"
          >
